  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 134 AM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nMoist southwest flow aloft continues on Thursday, with the best forcing\nremaining across the eastern Great Basin into western Colorado leading\naround round of heavy rain expected along and west of the ContDvd once \nagain, with the Flood Watch remaining in place for the eastern San Juan\nmtns and the Upper Rio Grande Valley. Scattered to numerous afternoon and \nevening showers and thunderstorms are expected once again for the rest of \nhigher terrain, with the potential for flash flooding if storms can move \nacross the recent burn areas. Further east, despite temperatures warming\ninto the upper 70s to upper 80s across the plains, latest models continue\nto suggest mid level warmth to keep most convection capped through the \nafternoon and evening.  \n\nFor Friday and Saturday, flow aloft becomes more westerly as the Pac NW \nupper trough translates across the Intermountain West and the Northern \nRockies through Saturday. While some drier air works across the region \nwithin the westerly flow, there looks to be enough uvv with embedded \npassing waves to support scattered to numerous afternoon and evening \nshowers and storms to all of south central and southeast Colorado, both \ndays. \n\nThere remains some differences in the amount of drying and location and\ntiming of the passing Northern Rockies wave for the weekend, with NBM \nkeeping cooler temperatures and good chances of showers and storms \nacross the area on Sunday and Monday, with potentially warmer and drier \nconditions developing under a building upper level ridge into the middle\nof next week.",
